[
{ type: install
  message: <<EOM
netman serves its web interface on http://127.0.0.1:8080/ by default.  Pass
--listen 0.0.0.0 to reach it from another host, and open the port in your
firewall accordingly.

The interface needs a browser with a working WebGL context, and on a machine
with no usable GPU the two common browsers do not behave the same way:
www/firefox works as installed, while www/linux-chrome must be started with
--enable-unsafe-swiftshader or the page hangs on "connecting" forever.  See
the WEB INTERFACE AND WEBGL section of netman(1) for the reason and for how
to tell this apart from a network problem.

Live capture reads the bpf(4) devices, so netman is normally run as root.
To let a non-root user capture instead, relax the permissions of /dev/bpf
with a devfs.rules(5) entry:

    [netman=10]
    add path 'bpf*' mode 0640 group netman

and enable it in /etc/rc.conf:

    devfs_system_ruleset="netman"

Note that read access to bpf(4) is enough to observe all traffic reaching
the interface, so grant it only to trusted users.

To run netman at boot, set in /etc/rc.conf:

    netman_enable="YES"
    netman_iface="em0"		# index or name substring, required
    netman_listen="127.0.0.1"	# 0.0.0.0 to allow access from the network
    netman_port="8080"

On a switched network you will only see your own traffic plus broadcast and
multicast.  Connect the machine to a SPAN/mirror port or a TAP to observe
more; netman stays passive by design.

The frontend files are installed in %%DATADIR%%/static, which is the
built-in default for --static-dir.
EOM
}
]
